From 099f56fc80331d01d98cdf779f70dbb397146bfe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 20:06:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/9] compat: replace the Qt5 only APIs that have a Qt4
 spelling

- crypto.cpp gets SHA-256 and HMAC-SHA-256 from libgcrypt on Qt4,
  QCryptographicHash learnt about SHA-256 in Qt 5.0 and
  QMessageAuthenticationCode is Qt 5.1.  libgcrypt is already a hard
  dependency through the internal lxqt_wallet backend.
- QStringList::join( QChar ) is Qt 5.0,use the QString overload.  That
  one bites twice: the obvious join( ',' ) call sites,and the two that
  pass a char variable.
- QDir::removeRecursively() is Qt 5.0,added
  utility::removeFolderRecursively() which forwards to it on Qt5 and Qt6.
- QFileDevice is Qt 5.0,the permission enumerators are reachable through
  QFile on every version.
- QSysInfo::currentCpuArchitecture() is Qt 5.4,the older code path now
  reports the architecture the binary was built for instead of reading a
  /proc file that does not exist.
- QStyleFactory::create() returns nothing for "Fusion" on Qt4,fall back
  to Plastique and never hand a null style to QApplication::setStyle().
- QHeaderView::setSectionResizeMode() is Qt 5.0,it was setResizeMode().
- QProcess::processId() is Qt 5.3,pid() is the older spelling.
- QProcess::arguments() is Qt 5.0.  processManager::Process already keeps
  the engines::engine::args it was built from,and exe->start() is handed
  that very same cmd_args list,so the accessor returns it directly.
- QAbstractScrollArea::setSizeAdjustPolicy() is Qt 5.2 and uic turns the
  .ui property into a call to it.  The table it was set on has a fixed
  geometry inside a fixed size dialog so the property had no effect
  anyway.

Two macro traps rather than APIs:

- favorites.h had "enum class STATES{ UNDEFINED,TRUE,FALSE }" and Qt4
  still carries the Qt3 compatibility macros TRUE and FALSE in qglobal.h,
  so the enumerators expanded to "true" and "false".  Renamed to
  IS_TRUE/IS_FALSE.
- Q_OS_MACOS is the Qt 5.9 spelling of Q_OS_MAC.  On Qt4 both
  "#ifdef Q_OS_MACOS" blocks in the tree would have compiled away and
  taken the macOS bodies of utility::platformIsLinux(),platformIsOSX(),
  platformIsWindows() and systemSignalHandler::addSignal() with them - a
  link failure,and a silent one to diagnose.  qt4compat.h aliases it and
  systemsignalhandler.cpp includes qt4compat.h before its first platform
  check.  A sweep of every object like macro qglobal.h defines on Qt4 but
  not on Qt5 turned up no other collision.

And Qt4 declares signals as protected,so debugWindow grew a public
showOutPut() that emits UpdateOutPut() for utility::logger to call.

The stragglers were found by diffing the member functions,and then the
overload signatures,that Qt 5.15 declares per class against what Qt 4.8
declares - rather than the method names alone.  A name present in both
versions with a narrower parameter type added in Qt5 is invisible to a
name only diff,and unioning all the headers together hides a name that
exists on another class.  join() is the only method in the tree where
the project passes the Qt5 only parameter type.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
